@node emacsctl REPL
@section emacsctl REPL

@example
emacsctl repl                  # elisp (default)
emacsctl repl --lang crispy    # inline C, compiled in the editor
emacsctl repl --lang bacon     # the editor's bacon shell
emacsctl repl --lang eshell    # the editor's eshell
emacsctl repl --lang calc      # the cmacs calculator
@end example

Every expression is evaluated @emph{inside the target cmacs} --- the
REPL sees the live editor state, and works identically over
@option{--host} (the whole session rides one ssh connection).

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .38 .25 .25
@headitem Language @tab Evaluates via @tab Multi-line rule @tab Result
@item @code{elisp}
@tab @code{org.cmacs.Editor1.Eval}
@tab balanced parens/strings
@tab printed value
@item @code{crispy}
@tab @code{Editor1.Crispy.EvalString}
@tab balanced braces
@tab captured stdout
@item @code{bacon}
@tab @code{Editor1.Bacon.Eval}
@tab one line
@tab output, @code{[exit N]} on failure
@item @code{eshell}
@tab @code{Editor1.Eshell.Eval}
@tab one line
@tab command output
@item @code{calc}
@tab @code{Editor1.Calc.Eval}
@tab one line
@tab computed result
@end multitable

Unbalanced input continues on a @code{...} prompt, so multi-line
defuns and C blocks paste naturally.  @code{calc} is deliberately
one-line: its input is infix, not s-expressions, so parens are
grouping/call syntax rather than a multi-line form --- an unbalanced
@code{sqrt(5} is an error the engine reports, not a request for a
continuation line.

When built against GNU readline (auto-detected) you get full
readline editing, with per-language history at
@file{~/.local/state/cmacs/emacsctl_history.<lang>}.  Without
readline --- or whenever stdin is not a tty --- input falls back to
plain line reading, which makes the REPL pipeable:

@example
printf '(emacs-pid)\n(emacs-uptime)\n' | emacsctl repl
@end example

The REPL is built on an abstract @code{CtlReplRuntime} GObject class
plus a name->factory registry; adding a language is one spec row +
one @code{ctl_repl_runtime_register()} call
(@pxref{Extending emacsctl}).
